  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1032 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains in place over the Southeast, with troughing \nover the West Coast. This will keep moist southwesterly flow in \nplace through the end of the week, maintaining an anomalously rich \nmoisture pool over eastern Utah and western Colorado. PWATs will \nhover around 200% of normal for mid-September through the end of the \nweek. This equates to PWAT values around 1.00 inches, which is more \nthan enough to produce heavy rain. The Weather Prediction Center \nkeeps a marginal risk of excessive rain across most of eastern Utah \nand western Colorado through at least Friday, with a slight risk of \nexcessive rain outlooked for areas around the Four Corners through \nThursday. As a result, the highest likelihood of flash flooding, \ndebris flows, and rockfalls will be for areas south of the I-70 \ncorridor, with lower chances across the north. \n\nAn upper level jet lingers over the Four Corners through Thursday, \nproviding some dynamic forcing and adding some wind shear. Where \nenough instability is present, this could result in strong to severe \nthunderstorms, capable of producing large hail and damaging winds. \nThe question, as always, becomes whether or not morning cloud cover \nwill hamper afternoon convection chances. Hi-Res models remain \nbullish on afternoon instability values Tuesday, with 1000-1500 J/kg \nof CAPE south of I-70, and 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE elsewhere. As a \nresult, we could see similar activity to that earlier today. \nWednesday is set up to be the big day at this point, with the best \nmoisture of the week, jet energy still in place, and a shortwave \ntransiting the region. There is potential for widespread heavy rain \nas a result, though the details will have to wait until the higher \nresolution model data comes in. \n\nModels indicate the upper level West Coast trough starting to push \neast on Thursday, nudging the moisture plume eastward. This will \nlead to a slight downturn in convection Thursday afternoon, but with \nplenty of surface based moisture still around, it will likely be \nanother rinse and repeat day of widespread afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms. Friday onward, forecast confidence decreases. Models \nare in poor agreement as to what happens once the West Coast trough \nejects to the northeast, with some solutions building a ridge, and \nothers developing a Rex Block over the Southwest. Ensembles do \nindicate a gradual drying trend, but keep PWATs of 100-150% of \nnormal around through the weekend. That's enough to keep a chance of \nat least an isolated, terrain based shower or storm in the forecast. \nWith abundant cloud cover and shower activity expected through the \nend of the week, temperatures will run near to around 5 degrees \nbelow normal.",
